# Item-specific findings from actual primary statements and, for non-iff axes,
# the relevant complete local proofs and inherited model definitions.
summaries={
'lem-dp-approximate-kernel-purification':'Under two approximate event bounds, the lemma constructs a pure density-ratio kernel within integrated total variation delta. It asserts existence from these hypotheses; it does not characterize approximate privacy by an iff involving purification.',
'lem-dp-test-function-extension':'The event domination hypothesis implies domination of integrals of every measurable unit-interval test function. This printed direction is the extension argument; no converse characterization is part of its Statement.',
'lem-exp-negative-argument-second-order-upper-bound':'The sole conclusion is the quadratic upper bound on the exponential for each nonpositive real argument. There are no two propositions joined by an equivalence to split into implication directions.',
'lem-finite-state-sup-norm-space-is-complete':'The claim constructs a metric on the finite-coordinate real function space and proves completeness, including the singleton empty-function branch. It does not characterize completeness by a reverse implication.',
'lem-linucb-gaussian-mixture-supermartingale':'The linear-bandit and positive regularization inputs imply that the displayed determinant-weighted exponential process is an integrable nonnegative supermartingale in the post-observation filtration. No converse from a supermartingale property to the bandit model is asserted.',
'lem-lmnn-explicit-subgradient-and-data-bound':'For fixed target/impostor lists the selected hinge coefficients produce a subgradient and a uniform data-dependent norm bound, including coefficient zero at a hinge kink. This is a construction and bound, not a subgradient iff characterization.',
'lem-local-gaussian-characteristic-function-is-global':'Equality with the Gaussian characteristic function on a neighborhood of the origin is a sufficient hypothesis for the standard Gaussian law. The printed implication does not separately assert the converse, even though a standard Gaussian of course has that characteristic function.',
'lem-negative-entropy-geometry-of-simplex':'The negative-entropy function in independent simplex coordinates is continuous, strongly convex, and has the stated attained extrema. These are properties of the given function, not an equivalence between alternative hypotheses.',
'lem-pac-mdp-adaptive-sample-concentration':'Potential streams realize adaptive interaction prefixes and give fixed-size reward, transition, and predictable-target tail bounds. No necessity or iff characterization of stream representations or concentration is stated.',
'lem-pac-mdp-value-simulation':'Uniform reward/transition perturbation bounds imply policy and optimal-value error bounds, with a separate trap-model sufficient-condition branch. The result does not infer model-parameter accuracy from a value error.',
'lem-pc-fisher-transform-uniform-concentration':'A nondegenerate Gaussian model, bounded population partial correlation, and positive residual sample degrees imply a Fisher-transform tail estimate with uniform constants. It is a sufficient concentration theorem, not a characterization of Gaussianity or a logical equivalence.',
'lem-policy-value-satisfies-bellman-equation':'The expected discounted value of the supplied stationary policy satisfies its Bellman equation. The Statement proves this fixed-point identity from the path-value definition and does not state a converse identification theorem here.',
'lem-positive-semidefinite-cone-is-frobenius-closed':'For positive matrix dimension the specified positive-semidefinite cone is closed in the symmetric Frobenius space. The quadratic-form description defines the cone; the asserted result is closedness, not an iff to direction-check.',
'lem-r-max-empirical-model-accuracy':'The selected sample threshold and value-iteration tolerance give simultaneous empirical-model, optimism, greedy-policy and update-count bounds. These sufficient algorithm guarantees have no asserted reverse implication from success to accurate models.',
'lem-symmetric-matrix-frobenius-isometry':'The diagonal/off-diagonal coordinate map is a linear bijection preserving Frobenius inner products and norms. This verifies properties of the supplied map rather than an iff classification of isometries.',
'lem-ucrl2-evi-termination':'The transfer routine attains the finite confidence-set optimum, and strictly positive radii imply span contraction and finite extended-value-iteration stopping. No converse saying every terminating iteration has positive radii is claimed.',
'lem-ucrl2-uniform-confidence-bounds':'The adaptive sample-tape model implies simultaneous empirical reward and transition confidence bounds over positive visit counts. This is a probability guarantee, not an equivalence relating confidence events to the sampling assumptions.',
'thm-advanced-adaptive-composition-standard-borel-outputs':'Per-history approximate privacy on Polish-isomorphic output spaces implies the stated advanced full-transcript privacy parameters. It does not characterize per-round privacy by the privacy of the composition.',
'thm-basic-and-advanced-composition':'The theorem derives basic and advanced full-transcript guarantees from per-history kernel privacy, in two parameter branches. It does not assert a reverse inference from a private transcript to prescribed per-round bounds.',
'thm-bellman-contraction-and-unique-fixed-point':'Each supplied finite-MDP Bellman operator is a gamma contraction and therefore has one fixed point, with the empty function space explicitly included. No converse characterization of Bellman operators or discounts is asserted.',
'thm-bellman-error-unlearnability-example':'The two explicitly specified reward processes have identical observable sequence laws but different Bellman-error objectives and, at positive discount, different minimizing parameters. This is a witness of nonidentification, not an iff claim about observability.',
'thm-communicating-mdp-common-optimal-average-reward':'Communicating finite bounded-reward dynamics imply a state-independent normalized finite-horizon value limit in the unit interval. The theorem does not claim the converse that a common limiting reward makes an MDP communicating.',
'thm-delayed-q-learning-pac-bound':'Legal positive-tolerance parameter choices imply a high-probability bad-step count, with separately stated zero-tolerance and uniform-parameter branches. The word Equivalently introduces an asymptotic reexpression up to logarithms, not a biconditional between model predicates.',
'thm-dp-postprocessing':'A private database kernel composed with a data-independent output kernel retains its privacy parameters. There is no converse asserting that privacy of a postprocessed output implies privacy of the original release.',
'thm-exp3-expected-regret':'Positive learning rates give procedure identities, and the tuned rate gives expected fixed-arm regret under oblivious or past-adaptive rewards. These are forward algorithm guarantees, not a characterization by an iff.',
'thm-explore-then-commit-expected-regret':'The positive exploration count and sub-Gaussian reward-stack hypotheses imply the displayed expected regret upper bound. No reverse inference from low regret to the reward law is included.',
'thm-exponential-mechanism-privacy-and-utility':'Finite positive utility sensitivity and a fixed nonempty finite output range imply privacy plus a finite-range utility tail bound. These are conclusions of the mechanism construction, not equivalent definitions of privacy or optimality.',
'thm-frobenius-projection-onto-psd-cone-is-spectral-clipping':'Spectral clipping of the given symmetric matrix uniquely minimizes Frobenius distance over the PSD cone and is independent of the eigenbasis. The theorem constructs the projection; it is not stated as an iff condition on arbitrary proposed matrices.',
'thm-hedge-static-regret':'The multiplicative-weight procedure yields a deterministic weighted-loss inequality and a tuned expected static-regret bound for bounded losses, with an explicit one-expert branch. No equivalence or converse hypothesis test appears.',
'thm-laplace-density-from-exponential-law':'A positive scale yields the displayed absolutely continuous Laplace law and its density. This is a representation theorem for the named law, not a biconditional characterizing all probability measures.',
'thm-laplace-mechanism-privacy':'Finite global query sensitivity and a positive privacy parameter imply pure privacy of the defined Laplace release. It does not infer sensitivity bounds from a privacy conclusion.',
'thm-linucb-regret':'The specified confidence radii, model and reward-width assumption imply a pseudo-regret bound; a separate paragraph proves procedure completion and operation counts. Neither guarantee is asserted as a converse characterization of the model or selector.',
'thm-mahalanobis-distance-is-pseudometric':'A given PSD symmetric matrix factors through a Euclidean linear image, implying the pseudometric axioms. The statement expressly does not assert separation, nor an iff from pseudometric properties to the supplied matrix assumptions.',
'thm-online-gradient-descent-regret':'The supplied feasible start and supporting-gradient inequalities imply a realization-wise regret bound, with separate zero-diameter or zero-gradient no-op branches. No reverse implication from small regret to convexity is claimed.',
'thm-pac-mdp-escape-event-reduction':'Simultaneous optimism, policy accuracy and a finite update/escape budget imply a PAC bad-time count. It does not assert necessity of these sufficient events or a reverse complexity characterization.',
'thm-pc-oracle-correctness':'The causally sufficient faithful perfect-oracle regime implies skeleton, collider and CPDAG correctness for a fixed-order PC run. Correct output is not asserted to imply the input regime, so there is no converse boundary.',
'thm-policy-improvement':'A greedy policy improves value, and the specified retain-current-action tie rule makes exact policy iteration terminate at the optimal value. These are forward sufficient conditions, not an iff between greediness and arbitrary value improvement.',
'thm-r-max-pac-bound':'The prescribed sampling and approximation parameters imply the R-MAX bad-step bound, with explicit zero-tolerance caveats and a uniform positive-tolerance form. The theorem does not characterize PAC performance by these parameter choices.',
'thm-scaffold-convex-and-nonconvex-rates':'The three separate convexity branches and their prescribed server-iterate output rules imply finite-round error and communication bounds. No reverse inference from those rates to strong convexity, ordinary convexity, or smoothness is asserted.',
'thm-square-ica-identifiability-at-most-one-gaussian':'Two normalized independent-component representations with at most one Gaussian component imply signed-permutation agreement. This is the identifiability implication and does not assert a converse characterization of independent representations.',
'thm-ucrl2-high-probability-regret':'Finite communicating dynamics and the normalized diameter imply two fixed-horizon realized-regret bounds, while the old all-horizon logarithm formula is explicitly refuted. Neither the repaired bounds nor that refutation states a biconditional.',
'thm-ucrl2-regret-lower-bound':'For every uninformed learner and admissible parameter tuple there exists a hard communicating environment. The minimax lower-bound direction and separately refuted old quantifier order do not form a logical equivalence.'}
noniff={
('lem-finite-state-sup-norm-space-is-complete','empty'):'The explicit empty-state metric branch has distance zero. Proof1.1 identifies the function space with its single empty function, checks the metric axioms on that singleton, and notes that every sequence is constant and convergent; it does not take a maximum of an empty set.',
('lem-pac-mdp-adaptive-sample-concentration','empty'):'The bounded-reward discounted model requires a nonempty finite state set and a nonempty finite action set at each state. A fixed admissible pair and a sample size m>=1 are inputs to these estimates; there is no empty tape-prefix denominator or empty state distribution in the claimed bounds.',
('lem-pac-mdp-value-simulation','empty'):'Both supplied models use the nonempty finite state/action convention of the bounded-reward discounted model. Thus the state supremum norm is a maximum on a nonempty finite set, and each Bellman action maximum in proof1.2 is over a nonempty action set; an empty model is outside the stated domain.',
('lem-pac-mdp-value-simulation','nonempty-choice'):'For the two given models the common action sets are finite nonempty sets. Proof1.2 uses the attained finite-maximum inequality to compare optimal Bellman backups; it never selects an element of an arbitrary infinite family of maximizers. The stationary-policy bound1.1 uses a supplied common policy. This review concerns those finite selections, not a new choice-free path-law construction.',
('lem-policy-value-satisfies-bellman-equation','empty'):'The Statement expressly includes empty states. Proof1.1 settles that branch as equality of the unique empty functions before defining start-state path expectations; no initial state or policy action is selected in that branch.',
('lem-policy-value-satisfies-bellman-equation','nonempty-choice'):'For nonempty states the Statement explicitly assumes AC, FactsA1 ties it to the path-space construction, and proof1.1 forms the start-state path laws using F2. The finite Bellman recurrence1.2 and dominated-limit step2.1 then use those given laws; this is not claimed to be a choice-free infinite-path construction.',
('lem-r-max-empirical-model-accuracy','empty'):'The inherited discounted model has a nonempty finite state set and nonempty statewise action sets, so S and A in the logarithm and sample threshold are positive. Proof1.1 unions over at most SA admissible pairs and proof1.2 allows the known set to be empty, but an empty known set is distinct from an excluded empty MDP.',
('lem-r-max-empirical-model-accuracy','nonempty-choice'):'The empirical-model lemma uses the fixed R-MAX procedure on finite nonempty action sets. In proof3.2 the current greedy action is the supplied procedure choice and finite action maxima exist; proof1.2 compares finite Bellman sweeps. No extra arbitrary-family selection is hidden in the update-count conclusion4.1.',
('lem-ucrl2-uniform-confidence-bounds','empty'):'The Statement requires at least one state, at least one available action per state through the model, and a positive integer horizon. Its empirical bounds are stated only for observed prefixes with1<=n<=T, so no empty-state probability vector or zero-length empirical average is evaluated.',
('lem-ucrl2-uniform-confidence-bounds','zero'):'Zero rewards and zero transition masses are allowed: proof1.1 places rewards and subset indicators in the closed unit interval, and proofs2.1–2.2 center these variables without dividing by their means. Zero visits are excluded by1<=n<=T rather than substituted into the square-root denominator; this corrects the old generic step1.1 credit.',
('lem-ucrl2-uniform-confidence-bounds','one'):'With one state the next-state empirical and true probability vectors are both the single mass1, so their L1 error vanishes. One action or one horizon step still leaves a nonempty finite pair/prefix union in proofs2.1 and3.1, and n=1 has a positive denominator. The actual proof uses no lower count beyond the printed positive-integer assumptions.',
('lem-ucrl2-uniform-confidence-bounds','endpoints'):'Reward endpoints0 and1 are included in the range used by proof1.1. Their centered width-one tape increments obey exactly the same two-sided Hoeffding estimate2.1; transition indicators also take these endpoint values in2.2. Confidence delta stays strictly between0 and1, so its excluded endpoints are not inserted into logarithms.',
('lem-ucrl2-uniform-confidence-bounds','degenerate'):'Deterministic rewards or deterministic next states produce constant tape coordinates, hence zero corresponding centered fluctuations. The width-one upper bounds and finite subset union in proofs2.1–3.1 remain valid without requiring nonzero variance or tie-free policy choices; the policy may be any admitted nonanticipating measurable rule.',
('thm-bellman-contraction-and-unique-fixed-point','empty'):'Proof1.1 identifies the two Bellman formulas on empty states with the unique empty self-map. Proof1.2 explicitly gives both sides of the contraction inequality as zero there, and proof2.1 applies fixed-point completeness to the nonempty singleton space of empty functions; no state maximum is taken.',
('thm-communicating-mdp-common-optimal-average-reward','zero'):'The all-zero reward model has V_n(s)=0 by the finite Bellman recurrence in proof1.1, so the limiting average is zero. More generally zero rewards and zero transition masses enter only nonnegative finite averages; proof3.1 divides by positive n in the limit, never by a reward or the raw diameter.',
('thm-communicating-mdp-common-optimal-average-reward','one'):'For a single state, the raw diameter is zero and proof1.1 gives zero span of each finite-horizon value vector. The maximum expected reward action exists on the finite nonempty action set; its repeated use yields the common scalar limiting gain. Proofs2.1–3.1 allow additive-defect bound D0=0.',
('thm-communicating-mdp-common-optimal-average-reward','endpoints'):'Both reward endpoints0 and1 are admitted. Proof1.1 uses nonnegativity for the pre-hit reward comparison and the upper bound1 to cap a lost suffix by its number of steps. Proofs2.1 and3.1 give0<=V_n(s)<=n, hence the closed limiting interval including gains0 and1; no strict reward bounds are used.',
('thm-communicating-mdp-common-optimal-average-reward','degenerate'):'Deterministic transition or reward kernels and tied maximizing actions do not break the recurrence in proof1.1: each finite action maximum is attained, and any attaining action supports the finite-horizon argument. The hitting-time comparison and almost-additive limit use only communication and bounded rewards, not positive variances or unique optimizers.',
('thm-delayed-q-learning-pac-bound','empty'):'The bounded-reward discounted-model convention excludes empty states and empty statewise action sets. Consequently SA>=1 and the finite update counts and logarithms in the positive-tolerance formulas have their stated domains. Zero bad-step count is permitted and separately handled; it is not an empty-model case.',
('thm-delayed-q-learning-pac-bound','nonempty-choice'):'The specified Delayed Q-learning procedure has a fixed action tie rule on each finite nonempty legal-action set. Proof1.3 uses its greedy policy and finite Bellman maxima, and proof2.1 passes it to the escape-event reduction. This row records finite policy selection, not a claim that the inherited path-law and conditional-expectation suppliers are choice-free.',
('thm-exponential-mechanism-privacy-and-utility','endpoints'):'The privacy and utility formulas explicitly require epsilon>0, finite positive utility sensitivity, and t>0. Thus epsilon=0 and sensitivity=0 are excluded rather than substituted into the denominator2Delta/epsilon; no closed-endpoint extension is asserted. The empty utility-tail event has probability zero in proof2.1 and is covered independently of these excluded parameter endpoints.',
('thm-laplace-density-from-exponential-law','endpoints'):'The scale domain is the open positive half-line b>0. Scale zero is excluded because the displayed density uses1/(2b) and the exponential-law construction has rate1/b; no finite endpoint infinity is a scale value. Proof1.1 separately covers the spatial point z=0 by its zero Lebesgue mass, so the density formula on all real locations is not missing an endpoint.',
('thm-laplace-mechanism-privacy','endpoints'):'The Statement requires epsilon>0; epsilon=0 is not substituted into the scale ratio. Zero query sensitivity is nevertheless covered by the separate deterministic release branch in proof3.1, while proof1.1 only sets b=Delta/epsilon in the strictly positive sensitivity branch. The old no-interval-parameter wording was too broad for these actual domain boundaries.',
('thm-online-gradient-descent-regret','empty'):'The feasible set is explicitly nonempty and the initial point x1 in K is supplied. This excludes empty projection/comparator domains before either the positive-step or no-op branch is used. Proofs can compare to every given u in K without choosing from an unspecified empty argmin; the static-regret conclusion separately requires an attained finite minimum.',
('thm-pac-mdp-escape-event-reduction','empty'):'The underlying discounted model requires nonempty finite states and statewise action sets, making V_t(s)=max_a Q_t(s,a) meaningful. The known set K_t and the update/escape event can be empty; proof1.1 still defines their dedicated known-model comparison, and the claim does not conflate those allowed empty subsets with an empty model.',
('thm-pac-mdp-escape-event-reduction','nonempty-choice'):'A fixed tie order and finite nonempty action sets define the deterministic greedy policy in the Statement. FactsA1 separately declares AC for history-dependent path laws, used in the coupling of proof1.1. The stopping-time trials3.1 and conditional argument4.1 therefore do not assert a choice-free path construction merely because the greedy action selection is finite.',
('thm-policy-improvement','empty'):'The Statement includes the empty-state policy-iteration branch. Proof1.1 makes all statewise inequalities vacuous and the unique empty policy unchanged; proof6.1 identifies its policy set with the singleton empty product, so the claimed zero changes and one evaluation do not require selecting an action.',
('thm-policy-improvement','nonempty-choice'):'For nonempty states, the Statement and FactsA1 explicitly assume AC for expected path values. Proof1.1 invokes those values and their Bellman identity. After initial policy and finite action lists are supplied, proof5.1 selects the earliest maximizing action while retaining already-greedy actions, and proof6.1 counts a finite product; no additional infinite selection is used by that iteration.',
('thm-r-max-pac-bound','empty'):'The inherited bounded-reward discounted model has positive state count and nonempty legal-action sets, hence A and SA in the thresholds are positive. Proof1.1 treats zero tolerance as a separate parameter branch, not an empty-domain convention; the empty set of bad times is allowed in proof1.2 when tolerance dominates the value cap.',
('thm-r-max-pac-bound','nonempty-choice'):'R-MAX supplies a fixed tie rule on finite nonempty legal-action sets; the current policy comes from that finite maximization. Proofs1.2 and2.1 apply the empirical-model and escape-event suppliers to this procedure. This review validates the finite choice interface and does not relabel those stochastic suppliers as a choice-free construction.',
('thm-scaffold-convex-and-nonconvex-rates','nonempty-choice'):'FactsA2 expressly assumes AC for the conditional-expectation-as-an-a.e.-class interface F8, and proof1.1–1.3 invokes both A2 and F8 in the three potential recurrences. Positive client count and1<=S<=N make the uniform finite client sample nonempty, while each stated output-index law is supplied independently. No choice-free general conditional expectation is claimed.'}
